For all designs I ask you to make, have them be beautiful, not cookie cutter. Make webpages that are fully featured and worthy for production.

By default, this template supports JSX syntax with Tailwind CSS classes, React hooks, and Lucide React for icons. Do not install other packages for UI themes, icons, etc unless absolutely necessary or I request them.

Use icons from lucide-react for logos.

This site must keep working with JavaScript disabled. Preserve the following when editing:

- The `prerender()` plugin in `vite.config.ts` renders `src/entry-server.tsx` into `dist/index.html` at build time, so the shipped page contains real markup instead of an empty `<div id="root">`. Keep it in the `plugins` array. It must stay a Vite plugin — deploy pipelines run `vite build` directly, so a separate `&& node script` step in the npm `build` script never runs and silently ships a blank page.
- Tailwind is compiled at build time (`tailwindcss` v3 + `postcss.config.js` + `tailwind.config.js`). Never replace it with the `cdn.tailwindcss.com` script: that CDN generates CSS by running JavaScript against the live DOM, so with scripting disabled the page renders completely unstyled. Tailwind v4 is deliberately avoided here — its native `lightningcss`/oxide binaries have repeatedly failed to install in this build environment.
- PostCSS is configured **inline** in `vite.config.ts` under `css.postcss`, and there is deliberately **no `postcss.config.js`**. Passing an object to `css.postcss` makes Vite skip its config-file search, so a stale or half-synced `postcss.config.js` left on disk is ignored instead of breaking the build. Do not recreate that file; add or change PostCSS plugins in `vite.config.ts`.
- `tailwindcss`, `postcss`, and `autoprefixer` must stay in `devDependencies`, in sync with `package-lock.json`. `src/index.css` is only `@tailwind` directives plus progressive-enhancement rules, so if Tailwind does not run the build emits a ~1 kB stylesheet instead of ~17 kB and every page — not just the no-JS one — renders as unstyled serif text. A log line `Unknown at rule: @tailwind` means exactly this; fix the config, never silence the warning.
- `Cannot find module 'tailwindcss'` means the package is not installed — run `npm install`. In the bolt/WebContainer editor this usually means `node_modules` is stale relative to a `package.json` that arrived from an external sync, so reinstall after any "update codebase with external changes". Never respond to it by removing Tailwind from the build, emptying the PostCSS plugin list, or switching to the CDN: each of those turns a loud, one-command build error into a silently unstyled production site. Dropping a package from `package.json` while it remains in `package-lock.json` also makes `npm ci` fail outright, so change both together.
- `src/main.tsx` hydrates existing markup (`hydrateRoot`) when `#root` already has children, and adds `js`/`js-ready` to `<html>`. Keep both.
- The bootstrap script in `index.html` adds `js` to `<html>` and removes it again at DOMContentLoaded if the bundle never ran. Keep it inline in `<head>`.
- In `src/index.css`, anything that depends on JavaScript is scoped to `.js`, and the no-JS header styling is scoped to `html:not(.js)`. Never hide content by default — only under `.js`.
- Navigation is plain `<a href="#section">` anchors and the mobile menu is driven by the `#menu-toggle` checkbox, not React state. Do not replace either with click handlers or `scrollIntoView()`.
